Chapter: ScopeTryRegion
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A binding made in a `trying` body does not escape that
 body: not into `falling back to`, not into `on failure`, and not past
 the `end`. Each of the three regions is its own lexical scope over the
 scope the try was written in.

 The decision this file records, because 2.23 asked for one. A fallback
 runs precisely when the body did NOT finish, so a body binding is, by
 construction, possibly-unwritten when the fallback reads it -- and the
 body re-runs from the top on every attempt, so even "the value" is not
 one value but one per attempt. Offering the fallback a name whose slot
 holds a partial attempt is offering it uninitialized memory with a
 friendly name on it. The three regions therefore do not share bindings.

 Nothing in the tree relied on the old behaviour: `trying` occurs in
 exactly one other source file (`try-smoke`), and no fallback there names
 anything from its body.

 The direction that made this visible is a MISCOMPILE, not a rejection,
 and the two halves disagreeing is what produced it. The type checker was
 already right -- `infer-act` is handed the untouched `env` for each
 region, so the fallback's `label` types as the global `Text`. The name
 resolver and the emitter were not: `Scope` is a `mutable` record and
 `scope-add-local` stores in place, so the body's `label` stayed
 resolvable; the emitter's locals are a flat prefix list that nothing
 popped, so `lookup-local` found the body's Integer slot. The checker
 said Text, the emitter loaded an Integer, and the concatenation below
 read that integer as a Text pointer.

Section: Probe

  probe-addr : Integer = 40016

  label : Text
  label = "GLOBAL"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    poke-byte probe-addr 0 7
    trying 1 times
      label <- peek-byte probe-addr 0
      print-line-uni ("body: " & show label)
      fail "nope"
    falling back to
      print-line-uni ("fallback: " & label)
    end
    print-line-uni ("after: " & label)
  end
